<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Songtsen Gampo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 605–649</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Songtsen Gampo was the emperor who built the Tibetan Empire in the early 7th century, ruling until 649 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> as his capital. He conquered Zhangzhung in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, married princesses from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and, by tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, and is credited with introducing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and a writing system based on Indian scripts. Tibetan tradition counts him as the first of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo.jpg" alt="Songtsen Gampo" width="320" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bhavna Sayana · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_King_Songtsen_Gampo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
